<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Browser vessel lift ~ the page paints, the vessel worker holds

The page's main thread paints the DOM and asks the person; the browser vessel runs on a worker's thread of its own, with its own memory, and holds every key, store and ceremony. Each worker runs on its own thread with its own memory, so a key the vessel worker holds never enters the page's heap. Row `B1` carries the build (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-order`>>, rank 1). The operator rules that the browser and the node found together (2026-11-05: //"Browser + Node is part of founding"//) and that every slice lands before the rite (2026-11-05: //"Land all before the rite, no 'build a now, b later' in early alpha."//).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-opening`>>
!! The opening ~ what the page holds, measured

''Dated baseline.'' This slot reads the span as measured at `3293533a4` (2026-11-05); the slices below change it, and every other slot states present-tense invariants.

`packages/lararium-web/index.html` loads `src/main.ts` as a page module, and `main.ts` calls `openBrowserVessel`; its `bootUnderLock` runs on the page's main thread and holds there:

* the Automerge `Repo` with its IndexedDB adapter (`open-browser-vessel.ts`), the relay WebSocket (`LarWSClientAdapter`), the fed gate and the share policy;
* `runFoundingCeremony` and `runApplyAdmitPayload`, the face proof and the verb registry;
* the persona-root seed: `loadBrowserPersonaRootSeed(…, wardRoute)` opens the ward on the page at founding;
* the atom seed (hex into `daemonAuth`), the fleet-join mints (`browser-fleet-join.ts`, also driven straight from `main.ts`);
* the unlocked custody tree key, in a module-level `Map` in `browser-custody.ts`;
* the IndexedDB keystore handles (`openVesselIdb` in `browser-vessel-identity.ts`).

At that baseline the passphrase and the ward route arrive from page form closures in `main.ts` and pass to `standBrowserCustody` and `openBrowserVessel`.

Off the page already: the daemon and wiki kernels, the per-act `ward.worker` (`browserWardSign` opens the root there for its one act), and the shared holder's daemon island. The shared holder holds no key by design and routes `custody:write` back to a `session:sealer` tab; `holder-routes` sends `cas:want`, `receipt:request` and `custody:write` to the owner tab. The browser builds no `CapabilityRecord`. Node and browser share `composeCoreVessel` (`browser-caps.ts`), but the browser builds its identity, its ceremonies and its dial outside it, and its daemon wrapper lacks the node's `authShore` and `resolveBinding` proxies (`open-browser-daemon-vm.ts`).

''Idempotence as measured.'' A reload repeats the custody unlock and the vessel composition; an identity loads when present, and a continuity marker refuses a missing one. Genesis finds the persisted document first; the board climb writes only missing entries. A founding interrupted before the bootstrap write runs again and replaces its archive, which proves nothing about the earlier side effects. A repeated fleet offer returns its kept request, a different pending offer refuses, and the same answer lands again until a booted vessel settles the pending row. Followers resend unanswered writes under their original transaction ids; whether every retried ask lands once stands unmeasured.

At that baseline the witness `tools/page-entry-witness.mjs` reads the page's reach: `main.ts` reaches `@automerge/automerge-repo`, `@lararium/keyhive`, `browser-custody`, `browser-vessel-identity` and `seed-wrap-prf`, each through `@lararium/browser`'s index; `ward-core` arrives as a type import alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-shape`>>
!! The shape ~ who holds what, on which thread

|! Thread |! Holds |
| the ceremony document's thread | the ask's form alone: no mirror, no plugins, a strict CSP; the secret's bytes leave it down a brokered port |
| the page's main thread | the DOM, the projection sink, the TW5 mirror, the ask forms, `navigator.storage.persist`, Worker construction alone (a substrate spawner) |
| the vessel worker's thread | the whole `bootUnderLock`: the Repo, IndexedDB, the relay, the custody tree key, founding and admit, the face proof, the verbs, the `CapabilityRecord`, every tab's session |
| the island threads (daemon, wiki) | as they run now; the SharedWorker house runs them on its own thread |
| the ward act worker's thread | one act; its rows arrive from the vessel worker over a transferred port, the route from the ceremony document |

The vessel worker stands as a SharedWorker house where the engine offers one and as a dedicated worker (the floor) where it offers none, chosen by capability, never by a flag.

''Page to vessel'' (the page-to-vessel port carries no `answer` bytes): `hello{tabSession}` · `control-event` · `set-surface` · `join:offer{text}` · `join:answer{text}` · `page:life{hidden|pagehide}` · `mirror:port` · `ceremony:port{askId, port}` (one end of a ceremony's channel, transferred unread) · `ceremony:refused{askId, refusal}`.

''Vessel to page'': `phase` · `frame` · `control-result` · `port-msg` · `ask{askId, kind: unlock|bind-first|clear|ward, act, words}` · `ask:settled{askId}` · `status{custody, seedRest, connection, did, wiki…}` · `join:request{text}` · `spawn{role}` (the page builds a Worker and transfers its port without reading it) · `ended{why}`.

''Ceremony to vessel'': on a fresh `MessageChannel` the page mints per ask, one end transferred to the vessel worker and the other to the service worker, which hands it to the ceremony document's claim (`ceremony:offer` · `ceremony:claim` · `ceremony:port`). The vessel sends the public ask down it (`ask{askId, kind, act, digest, face, routes, passkeys, words}`); the ceremony answers `answer{askId}`, its bytes on a transferred `ArrayBuffer` (a passphrase's UTF-8, or a passkey's credential id and PRF output), so they detach from the sender and the input clears; the vessel answers `settled` or `refused`. Answers travel ceremony to vessel and never touch the surface page (`packages/lararium-web/src/ceremony-protocol.ts`).

''Typed payloads ~ nothing sovereign crosses to the page.''
* `ask{askId, kind, act}` carries a public act label and its digest alone, never act data: no route, passphrase, key, seed or row.
* `status{…}` carries public readings alone; `seedRest` stands as a public enum (`absent | wrapped-prf | wrapped-passphrase | open`), a name of the seed's resting state and never its bytes.
* `join:offer{text}` and `join:answer{text}` carry the public admission carriage alone: the offer or answer text a person copies between devices (the address, the public keys and the signed claims of the admit). It holds no route and no key.

''The passphrase path.'' The ceremony document (see #/the-ceremony) turns the passphrase into bytes and sends them down a port to the vessel worker (or to the ward act worker for a ward act), keeping nothing. The surface page never holds the bytes.

''The session record.'' Each tab's port carries one session; the session record holds its face as a field of the session, never of the port, so a session that changes face later (row `B8`) moves one field and keeps its port. The one vessel worker per house holds DISTINCT per-tab sessions on the floor: each admits through its own unlock, and no unlock and no key passes between tabs. A follower's brokered port maps to its own session record: the broker hands the follower's ceremony document a port minted for that tab's session id, so the answer that arrives on it admits that session alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-ceremony`>>
!! The ceremony ~ ruled by the operator

The road rules that the browser vessel "takes an unlock ceremony the surface cannot paint, the boundary sitting where the person types the secret (its shape stays research)". The surface page's own form therefore never takes the passphrase. The operator approved the house's default (2026-11-05: //"Approve"//), which closes the ceremony shape that dreamdom lists among its open rulings (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom`>> #/open-rulings): a clean ceremony document takes the secret.

* a same-origin page that runs no mirror, no plugins and a strict CSP, painting only the ask;
* it takes the secret and sends its bytes to the vessel worker down a `MessagePort` the service worker brokers (measured working in Chromium and Firefox, the probe at `~/.lares-scratch/b1-lift/probe/`);
* the surface page never holds the bytes;
* on a phone the ceremony reads as a navigation there and back.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-slices`>>
!! The slices ~ every one lands before the rite

Each slice lands red-first with its control. The sizes read as house estimates (S, M, L).

# ''Slice 0, the red witnesses (S).'' The page-entry witness walks `main.ts`'s import graph (static and dynamic imports, workspace names to their `src` entries, `?worker&url` spawns and type-only imports not reached) and refuses any reach into `@automerge/automerge-repo`, `@lararium/keyhive`, `browser-custody`, `browser-vessel-identity`, `ward-core` or `seed-wrap-prf`. Red: it fails on the span as it stands, so the register `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/unbuilt-laws.test.ts` declares it as THE PAGE REACHES NO VESSEL MODULE until slice 2 makes it hold.
# ''Slice 1, the substrate seams (S to M), built.'' The identity continuity rests in an IndexedDB row of its own database (`lares:continuity`, keyed by the keystore's name), apart from every keystore, so a deleted keystore still refuses a blank re-found (`continuity-lost`); the `localStorage` marker burns. `clearBrowserCustody` already asks through its `ConfirmDoor` parameter and reaches no page `confirm()`. The page feeds its hide and leave through `BrowserVesselOptions.pageLife` (the `page:life` message to come), which drives the wiki drains and the shared holder's session close, and takes the rest reading through `onRest` (a `status` field to come); the page's own `page-life.ts` subscribes the window and the document and paints `data-lar-rest`. Red (`vessel-worker-seams.test.ts`): the vessel's module graph loads on a dedicated worker's thread with no `window`, `document`, `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` and a `location` stripped to its `href`, and the identity founds, reloads the same key and refuses a lost keystore; a census over the package's source finds no page-global reach. It failed at continuity (`localStorage is not defined`). The named residual: a clear of every IndexedDB database on the origin takes the continuity row with the keystore.
# ''Slice 1b, the ceremony document (M), built.'' The clean ceremony document of #/the-ceremony stands, and the surface page paints no secret form. `packages/lararium-web/ceremony.html` stands as its own Vite entry with its own script (`src/ceremony/`), a strict policy in its meta (`default-src 'none'`, `script-src 'self'`, no `unsafe-inline`, no `unsafe-eval`, no form target) and in its response header with `frame-ancestors 'none'` (the preview server and the hearth's Pronaos both send it; the Pronaos carries `/ceremony.html` and `/ceremony-broker.js` as two fixed `no-store` routes held to the build's receipt). The flow: the vessel worker raises `ask`; the page paints the ask's public words and one button; the button mints a fresh `MessageChannel`, transfers one end to the vessel worker (`ceremony:port`) and the other to the service worker (`public/ceremony-broker.js`, registered at the floor), and opens the ceremony document as a window with no opener (`#ask=<id>`), so the page keeps no end of the channel and no handle to the window. The ceremony document claims its end from the broker (the broker hands a port only to a claim from `/ceremony.html`, holds it only between offer and claim, never starts or reads it, and handles no fetch), reads the ask the vessel sends down it (the public label and its digest, the face, the kind, the routes it offers), and answers there. Answer kinds: a passphrase (UTF-8 bytes on a transferred `ArrayBuffer`, detached at once, every field cleared; the vessel decodes and zero-fills its copy) for custody and the ward; a WebAuthn PRF output over a passkey pin's salt for a custody unlock or clear alone (the ward and a first bind offer the passphrase alone, and a passkey answer to a ward ask refuses `ward-passkey-refused`; a route the ask did not offer refuses `ceremony-route-refused`). The passkey slot binds beside `keyslot.ts`'s passphrase and paper (`browser-custody.ts` `CustodyBindRoute`, `readBrowserCustodyPasskeys` for the ceremony's assertion); a first seal or a first bind under a passkey alone refuses `passkey-alone-refused`. Refusals by name, never a fallback to a surface form: `ceremony-needs-secure-context`, `ceremony-framed`, `ceremony-csp-absent` (a string compiles as code: the policy stands out of force), `ceremony-broker-absent`, `ceremony-ask-absent`, `ceremony-ask-unbrokered`, `ceremony-ask-mismatch`; a page that cannot stand the broker names its refusal to the vessel, and the vessel's ask rejects by that name. The page's ward asks stand on: every ward act asks its passphrase through the ceremony, and the per-act ward worker stands its listener before it loads its core (its bundled graph awaited at its top level and lost the port's message). The ceremony carries its own reach witness, `tools/ceremony-entry-witness.mjs`: the ceremony's graph reaches its own directory and the protocol's types alone, with no package, mirror, TW5, plugin or keyhive; its document's policy holds; the hearth's header welds to the meta policy; its broker imports nothing. Reds: `@lararium/web` `tests/ceremony.test.ts` C1 to C4 (the ward refuses a passkey by name; the passphrase leaves detached; the four refusals; the page keeps neither port end), `tests/vessel-port.test.ts` (the page posts no answer), `@lararium/browser` `tests/browser-custody-passkey.test.ts` BP1 and BP2, `@lararium/node` `tests/pronaos-ceremony.test.ts` PC1 and PC2, the witness's self-test, and `tests/e2e/ceremony.e2e.test.ts` K1 and K2 on a walk build in a real Chromium: at the floor the document and its broker answer, the header forbids framing and a same-origin frame stands blocked; a fresh vessel founds (bind-first), reloads and unlocks, and signs one ward act through the ceremony (the signature verifies under the key it names), while a page-thread spy on every worker, port, service-worker and window message and every DOM input reads no byte of the route and the page stands no password field. The named residuals: a passkey slot in the custody tree refuses every browser ward act `ward-passkey-refused` until the browser root moves under a ward key of its own (seat-harness `S2b`); the passkey slot's bind door stands while no ceremony mints a credential (`navigator.credentials.create` with the PRF extension waits on its measurement on target devices); a phone's navigation there and back, and the face the ask names (null at the floor: "this browser"), ride slices 3 and 4; the broker stands as no boundary between same-origin scripts.
# ''Slice 2, the move (L), built.'' A dedicated `vessel.worker.ts` (`packages/lararium-web/src/workers/`, a Vite `?worker&url` beside its siblings) initializes Keyhive's WASM and runs `openBrowserVessel` unchanged (`vessel-host.ts`); the `BrowserVesselOptions` callbacks map one to one onto the typed messages of `vessel-protocol.ts`; `main.ts` talks to a `vesselPort` (`vessel-port.ts`) alone, paints frames, feeds the mirror's port road, asks `persist()` (page-only) and feeds its life signals. As built, page to vessel: `hello{tabSession, boot}` (public inputs: URLs, the served seed, the arrival's carriage) · `ceremony:port{askId, port}` · `ceremony:refused{askId, refusal}` · `control-event` · `set-surface` · `port:place` and `port:close` (the mirror's port road, the target's `mirror:port`) · `page:life` · `read:mirror-inputs` · `read:floor` · `join:offer{text}` · `join:answer{text}` · `spawned{port}`; vessel to page: `phase` · `frame` · `control-result` · `coherence` · `port-msg` · `port:refused` (read on the page as the binding's own refusal) · `ask{askId, kind, act, words}` · `ask:settled{askId}` · `status{custody, seedRest, connection, rest, vessel}` · `reply` (the reads and the join answer, the target's `join:request`) · `spawn{role}` · `ended{why}`. The boot's custody step, the fleet join's request, landing and settle, the seed-rest reading and the did run on the vessel worker. The islands spawn nested from the dedicated worker from the URLs the page hands over. `?holder` keeps working until slice 4: a dedicated worker builds no SharedWorker, so the vessel worker asks the page to build it (`spawn{role}`) and bridges its transferred port, which the page never reads. The page loads `@lararium/browser/page` alone. A custody or ward ask's answer rides the ceremony document's own port (slice 1b); the fleet join's admit carriage rides the tab's stash and a reload until #/what-dissolves takes it. Red: law ⑬ (THE PAGE REACHES NO VESSEL MODULE, `tools/page-entry-witness.mjs`) failed on five reaches through `@lararium/browser`'s index and holds, with the mesh barrel's `automerge-repo` reach cut on the way; `tests/e2e/vessel-worker.e2e.test.ts` failed on "the page builds the vessel worker" and passes: the built page founds a fresh vessel through the worker, the did row and the first paint read, the mirror shows, and the window's own resource timeline names no vessel chunk.
# ''Slice 3, custody in the vessel (M).'' Each port carries a session its tab admits by its own route; the face's last session closing drops the key; sealing runs on the vessel worker's thread. Each session admits through its own unlock, sharing none and no key with another tab. The last-tab transition: the face's last session closes, the tree key drops, and the browser stands at its floor. Store ownership: one Web Lock per store, the vessel worker holds it, and a second worker refuses by name (`store-owned`). The vessel worker holds a per-face custody key (FCK) map and per-face slot trees, and every `ask` and `clear` carries a `face` (the PersonaGroup, never `h<N>`). The vessel worker lights ONE Keyhive provider per face at the face's first unlock (K19: one provider per unlocked joined face; a face's lock and unlock ride `daemon:face-drop` and `daemon:face-lit`, with no clock and no recompose); each later tab of that face proves its own unlock to admit its own session, and the face's LAST session closing drops the provider and its keys. A session acts only as the face it wears, booked by request id. The per-tab session record stays in memory (the owner persists no roster of sessions). The last-session transition calls the holder's lock, which overwrites every key and ends an act in flight (each write checks the lock before it runs). An abrupt tab loss sends no close, so the last-session drop also rides port loss or the holder's own local monotonic liveness budget (its own liveness alone), never `page:life` alone. "The asker seals" keeps `seal-awaits-session`: only another admitted, unlocked session of the same face may seal when the asker cannot answer, and the dispatcher's local-verb mark binds to the session id. A bare key crosses no worker port and reaches no disk. The browser leaf's ember row (one secret per hearth, never synced, lost on eviction, answers signed asks) rides the leaf's custody slot, in this slice's keystore and ask path. A browser leaf holds a passphrase or PAPER slot before it holds a persona root: every boot rests at the floor until a human unlocks, and the root mint refuses `no-custody-slot` until a slot binds and unlocks before the vessel composes. A named floor reading serves the locked vessel worker (public bytes only, no sovereign signature, `signer-unavailable` on asks). A session signs, or nothing mints (the session signer rides slice 5a). Reds: of two tabs, B without an unlock reads no sealed row; after every tab closes, no tree key stands and reopening asks again; a second worker opening a held store refuses with `store-owned`; K15's isolation red runs inside the worker (unlock face A, bypass the router, face B's rows still refuse); Wave B's stranger's-port red (a port with no admitted session refuses by name) with its same-face controls (another session of the same face still reads, and an admitted same-face port receives its body); iOS Home Screen and Android WebAPK lifecycle reds (the asker killed mid-write; relaunch starts at the floor and asks again). An installed app or worker extends no authority: the SharedWorker's lifetime never stands in for authority. Ceremony and custody reds run on scratch roots with synthetic names and secrets.
# ''Slice 3b, sealed-store step three (L, sub-lane `b1-seal`).'' The document store's values seal as `change` and `checkpoint`; hydration checks against the session's face (a face change, row `B8`, re-checks it against the session's new face); the browser island's rest and the holder seat follow. It lands before the rite. Steps four (the DreamNet carriage protocol between vessels) and five (a public bag at a locked node's floor) belong to the sealed-store road and keep their own rows and rank (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>). Red: a session of face A hydrates no value sealed under face B, and no unsealed value reaches a store write.
# ''Slice 4, the SharedWorker house and the floor's port broker (M to L).'' The SharedWorker hosts slice 2's body, the dedicated worker serves as the floor, and `?holder` goes. On the floor, a follower tab reaches the leader's dedicated vessel worker through a `MessagePort` brokered for it, by the service worker or by the vessel worker minting a per-tab port; the broker hands the port over and never carries its traffic, so the follower's ceremony document sends its bytes straight down its own port. The Pronaos route inventory declares every new route (the ceremony document, the service worker and its scope, `vessel.worker.ts` and the SharedWorker) under a CSP delivery policy; a phone cannot hash-check a top-level page, so the ceremony's trust rests on the origin. The broker carries no content key, Web Lock `steal` stays forbidden (`ifAvailable` refuses loud), and the broker's handoff never stands as the sw-shell readiness signal: readiness waits on the service worker controlling the page. Red: a second tab on an engine with no SharedWorker admits its own session by its own unlock, and no page's heap ever holds the other tab's bytes.
# ''Slice 5a, the capability record (S, rides slice 3).'' The browser answers island asks through a `CapabilityRecord`: receipt, cas and dyad at construction, `custodyWrite` lifted on unlock. Red: a browser herm refuses `session:sign` with `signer-unavailable`, as `composeHerm` does.
# ''Slice 5b, one composer (L).'' One `composeVessel(substrate, floor, lifts)` serves the node and the browser; roles read as predicates over the record; one `LarVessel` type stands for both. Red: the browser and the node compose through the same function and answer the same capability probes alike.
# ''Slice 6, idempotence (M).'' A reload mid-ask; a retried ask (`askId` as the digest of act, session and nonce, never a counter); a crashed vessel worker (respawn, re-ask, same heads); a founding killed between `keepBrowserFaceAtomSeed` and the bootstrap write. Red: each lands once and reaches the same heads. A respawned vessel reissues `ownerEpoch` and fresh session ids, and no tab trusts an earlier greeting. The G4 txId ledger rests durably, so a retried write lands once across a respawn (red: a write the old island applied without its answer, sent again to the respawned island, lands once).
# ''Slice 7, the vessel reads its own durability (M; a house decision the operator may overrule).'' The vessel reads `persisted()` and the engine's storage class and shows the durability on the page through `status`; a founding or a join on storage that may get erased names that plainly before it binds (for one: //"this browser may forget this device after 7 days unless you add it to your Home Screen"//); the page forwards the `persist()` request, since only the page offers it. A vessel whose keystore vanished boots as a new vessel by name (a fresh per-face tag, a fresh join), never as a crash. Durability status distinguishes a requested drain or an in-memory flush from a storage-backed acknowledgement and a confirmed projection hash. Red: a stubbed storage class, not persisted and erasable, shows the warning, a wiped keystore boots as a new vessel by name, and a drain request alone never reads as a storage-backed acknowledgement.
# ''The X0b sweep over the browser vessel (M).'' A replay, a reboot and a retry land nothing new: every row names itself by its content or its causal id, and a re-run founding reaches the same end rather than minting a fresh atom and group that orphans the earlier rows. Red: a founding run twice from the same floor leaves one atom, one group and no orphan row.

''The pre-rite gate ~ joined founding.'' Slice 5b and the X0b sweep stand inside this gate. Red (e2e): a joined node and browser found end to end: the node founds through `lares vessel found`; the browser founds or joins through the lifted vessel and the ceremony document; both boot and see each other. The gate folds the real-device probe into founding-runbook step 8's `tools/capability-probe/` phone walk (a pre-rite gate item), with lifecycle reds; no seed crosses `join:offer` or `join:answer`; each device mints its own key in the worker; the ceremony refuses by name on an insecure origin.

The order follows the dependencies: 0, 1, 2, 1b, 3 with 5a, 3b, 5b, 6, 7, then the X0b sweep, then the pre-rite gate, and the rite follows the gate.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-dissolves`>>
!! What dissolves outright

* the `sessionStorage` admit stash and its `location.reload()`: the vessel worker re-enters its boot inside itself;
* the `session:sealer` announce and the route to a tab;
* the page listeners `onLock: lockBrowserCustody` and `onBrowserCustodyLock`;
* `houseOfBoot`'s page reads of IndexedDB;
* the split where a first founding takes the dedicated floor and the house stands on the next boot;
* `standBrowserCustody`'s pre-boot page step and `offerBootRetry`'s re-boot;
* the tab topology's leader, follower and lend, wherever a SharedWorker stands;
* `?holder`;
* `holder-routes`' owner routing for the four asks;
* the page's `readBrowserCustody` and `readBrowserSeedWrap` status reads;
* the browser ward's named residual moves to the ceremony document's form alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-founding-path`>>
!! The founding path after the cut

# The page connects its port; the vessel worker reads its stores and stands the floor (the place key).
# The vessel asks `bind-first`; the ceremony document takes the secret and sends the bytes down its brokered port; the slot binds and the root mints under the ward from the same route, zeroed after.
# The ceremony mints the atom, the anchor and the bootstrap; the daemon island builds with its record; frames reach the mirror.
# The join: `join:offer` raises an ask and answers `join:request`; `join:answer` hands the vessel the admit, which it lands and re-enters.
# The node founds through `lares vessel found`, and the crossing dials from the vessel worker.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/constraints`>>
!! Constraints ~ the risks, the kept laws, the engine facts to probe

''Risks.''
* Android Chrome's SharedWorker stands unmeasured, so the floor (the dedicated worker with its port broker) carries the rite wherever the house cannot stand.
* Chromium's SharedWorker constructs no nested Worker (Firefox's does), so no design rests on SharedWorker nesting; the holder runs its islands on its own thread for that reason.
* Keyhive's WASM initializes on the vessel worker's thread (`worker-boot.ts`).
* Vite's `?worker&url` resolves differently for nested spawns.
* People's plugins in the full mirror share the page's main thread with the form, so the full-mirror gate (sealed-store steps 2 and 3) stays.
* The holder's teardown `setTimeout(finish, 5_000)` lets a clock decide; the cure waits on the ack or the lock's release.
* `WARD_ATTEMPTS_PER_PAGE` resets on reload; the vessel worker counts per session instead.
* A re-run founding mints a fresh atom and group and orphans the earlier rows until the X0b sweep lands.

''Carried constraints, every slice.''
* The secure-context wall: a service worker and WebAuthn need a secure context, so a plain `http://192.168.x.x` page refuses by name.
* `seedRest=open` names the pre-bind window alone (only the place seed and the veil Archives stand clear before a slot binds; `bind-first` names that window and its close); PRF-wrapped custody replaces the cleartext resting form.
* The ward stays passphrase-only: never a passkey, one act one unlock, no route or key cached between acts, zeroed after use.
* The broker stands as no boundary between same-origin scripts: hostile same-origin code can take a Web Lock or approach the SharedWorker, and the `store-owned` refusal stays loud.
* A face switch reads as the five-step fence that advances a generation (row `B8`); each session binds one (face, Nexus), and a face hint grants nothing.
* SharedWorker lifetime under Safari's 7-day rule stays unmeasured on Safari and Firefox.
* Two faces on one device project two folders, each session leasing its own wiki; a locked vessel or a torn row loads no promoted code and never falls back to stage bytes; the board watcher stops at session close and at the last-tab transition; the vessel's seed self-consistency check against the hearth's arrival descriptor keeps its named mismatch refusals.
* Minted `blob:` URLs stay per session and out of persisted CRDT and disk records.
* No HTTP or RPC coordinator surface, and no browser singleton authority rooted in ambient page boot (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/browser/full-detached-worker-authority-pool-sprint`>>).
* The page keeps the G4 `mirror:began` `sessionStorage` record; any no-`sessionStorage` census exempts the page.

''The kept laws, every slice.'' No key on a surface; each tab's own unlock; the last tab returns the browser to its floor; one owner per store (a Web Lock); one Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup; refusals by name; red-first.

''Engine facts, measured'' (headless Chromium 1248 and Firefox 1555, the probe at `~/.lares-scratch/b1-lift/probe/`). A dedicated worker nests module workers that open IndexedDB. A WebSocket runs in dedicated and shared workers. A service worker brokers a `MessagePort` from tab A's dedicated worker to tab B; a `BroadcastChannel` carries no port (`DataCloneError`). A transferred `ArrayBuffer` detaches in the sender. Web Locks work in workers and block across tabs. WASM instantiates in a worker. `navigator.storage.persist()` stands on the page alone, while workers read `persisted()` and `estimate()`, so the page forwards the persist request.

''Engine facts, sourced'' (MDN browser-compat-data). Chrome Android ships SharedWorker only from 148, so a phone below it takes the service-worker broker. Safari ships SharedWorker from 16 and nested workers partly from 16.4, never inside a SharedWorker (webkit.org/b/265263; Chromium crbug 40695450). Web Locks arrive in Safari 15.4 and Chrome Android 69. Safari's tracking prevention deletes script-writable storage, IndexedDB included, after 7 days of Safari use without an interaction with the site, Home Screen web apps excepted (webkit.org/tracking-prevention), and Safari grants `persist()` by heuristic (webkit.org/blog/14403); slice 7 answers both.

''Engine facts, unmeasured and named.'' iOS terminating a service worker mid-handoff (the broker's handoff stays retryable and never assumes a live service worker); a port transferred through `client.postMessage` on iOS; iOS suspending a background tab's workers; WASM memory caps in iOS workers. A real-device probe on an iPhone and on an Android phone closes these before the rite.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/house-readings`>>
!! House readings ~ one ruled, the rest the operator may overrule

# ''The ceremony boundary (ruled by the operator, 2026-11-05: //"Approve"//).'' The clean ceremony document of #/the-ceremony: same origin, no mirror, no plugins, a strict CSP; it takes the secret and sends its bytes down a service-worker-brokered port to the vessel worker, and the surface never holds them.
# ''Every tab carries its own live session'' (operator, 2026-11-05: //"Multiple tabs will carry live sessions, and later can 'change face' (ONE FACE per session later on the roadmap)."//). Each tab's session wears one face and stands admitted by its own unlock. On the floor, a follower tab reaches the leader's dedicated vessel worker through a port brokered for it; the broker hands over the port and never carries its traffic, so no page relays any passphrase; the answer travels ceremony document to vessel. A session changing face rides row `B8`.
# ''One vessel worker per house.'' The page learns the house only as an opaque digest.
# ''The seal (the operator's default, pending).'' The ruling "the asker seals" (2026-10-19) rewords to "the asker's session seals, on the vessel worker's thread, while that face's session stands unlocked"; the `seal-awaits-session` wait stays.
# ''Same-face tabs (the house's default, overrulable).'' The vessel worker lights ONE Keyhive provider per face at the face's first unlock; each later tab proves its own unlock to admit its own session; the face's LAST session closing drops the provider and its keys (session-contract keeps its per-face provider line and the per-tab-sessions ruling).
# ''Passkey and PRF (the house's default, overrulable).'' The ward refuses passkeys (seat-harness); custody admits a PRF wrap that never stands as the sole copy (secure-context). The ceremony document hosts a passphrase answer kind (ward and custody) and a WebAuthn PRF answer kind (custody only); a passkey slot gets built; the ceremony document carries its own reach witness.
# ''Sealed-store step three joins the lift (the house's default, overrulable).'' It becomes slice 3b on sub-lane `b1-seal`, before the rite; steps four and five stay on the sealed-store road.
# ''The txId ledger rests durably (the house's default, overrulable).'' Slice 6's lands-once red then holds across a respawn.
# ''Pending operator ruling (DEFAULT PENDING; it changes admission bytes).'' Each device's ward public key rides its signed admission, and a ward-act approval carries that key's signature; the browser gains its own answer door.

<<~/ahu>>

